<!--
  The bento/tray mark, from tray/assets/tray-icon.svg.

  The navy frame is the BACKGROUND layer and the cream tray is the foreground.
  That split is what an adaptive icon wants — the system parallaxes the two
  layers against each other, and a frame drawn into the foreground would slide
  away from its own edge.

  The tray is 56 wide on the 108 canvas, not the full 72 safe zone. At 72 it
  exactly filled the visible area, so every launcher mask cut its corners off and
  the navy frame — the thing that makes the mark read as bento — was never drawn
  at all. At 56 the frame survives a circle mask, which is the tightest one in
  use, and the tray still sits inside the 66dp guaranteed-visible circle apart
  from its own rounded corners.

  Rounded rects are paths because Android vector drawables have no <rect>.
  Regenerate rather than editing the numbers — the snippet is in tray/README.md.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path
        android:fillColor="#F0EBE0"
        android:pathData="M37.39,26 H70.61 A11.39,11.39 0 0 1 82,37.39 V70.61 A11.39,11.39 0 0 1 70.61,82 H37.39 A11.39,11.39 0 0 1 26,70.61 V37.39 A11.39,11.39 0 0 1 37.39,26 Z" />
